 * **Checking an Element Type:** You can check an element's type with `element.type()`. It
   returns an `element_type` with values such as `simdjson::dom::element_type::ARRAY`, `simdjson::dom::element_type::OBJECT`, `simdjson::dom::element_type::INT64`,  `simdjson::dom::element_type::UINT64`,`simdjson::dom::element_type::DOUBLE`, `simdjson::dom::element_type::STRING`, `simdjson::dom::element_type::BOOL` or, `simdjson::dom::element_type::NULL_VALUE`.
 * **Output to streams and strings:** Given a document or an element (or node) out of a JSON document, you can output a minified string version using the C++ stream idiom (`out << element`). You can also request the construction of a minified string version (`simdjson::minify(element)`). Numbers are serialized as 64-bit floating-point numbers (`double`).
+* **Pretty printing:** Given a document or an element (or node) out of a JSON document, you can request the construction of a human-readable string version with `simdjson::prettify(element)`. The indentation is fixed to four spaces per nesting level. Numbers are serialized as 64-bit floating-point numbers (`double`).
+
+For example, given the JSON document `   [ 1 , 2 , 3 ] `, the following code:
+
+```c++
+dom::parser parser;
+dom::element doc = parser.parse("   [ 1 , 2 , 3 ] "_padded);
+cout << simdjson::prettify(doc) << endl;
+```
+
+prints:
+
+```
+[
+    1,
+    2,
+    3
+]
+```
+
+The `simdjson::prettify` function works with `element`, `array` and `object` instances, and with a `simdjson::simdjson_result<T>` when exceptions are enabled. Empty arrays and objects are printed on a single line (`[]` and `{}`).

